            <ArticleTitle>Post-traumatic intrahepatic splenosis mimicking a neuroendocrine tumour.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1136/bcr-2012-007885</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">bcr2012007885</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 52-year-old man presented with abdominal pain with a background of splenectomy 25 years previously. Initial investigations lead to suspicion of a neuroendocrine tumour. Positron emission tomography octreotide scanning and chromogranin were raised. He subsequently underwent a lateral segmentectomy. The histopathology was consistent with splenosis. 1. Splenosis must be considered as differential in any patient with abdominal symptoms post-traumatic splenectomy. 2. Positron emission tomography (PET) octreotide scanning can detect splenosis giving false positives for a neuroendocrine tumour. This is the first case to describe such an association.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
